\chapter{Compile-Time Dispatch: \code{match}, \code{when}, \code{otherwise}}
\label{ch:pattern-match}

\section{What dispatch means here}

Almost every language implementation eventually needs a table that maps a
value to a behaviour. A state machine maps a state to a transition
function. A command-line parser maps an option name to a handler. A
diagnostic engine maps an error code to a message. A lexer maps a token to
its classification.

In \cpp{} this is usually written as a \code{switch}, and a \code{switch} has
two well-known shortcomings. It is verbose: each arm repeats the case
pattern. And it is not extensible: adding a case means editing the
\code{switch}, which means the table cannot be assembled from independent
pieces.

The pattern-match facility addresses both. A table is built from a list of
\emph{clauses}, each of which pairs a key with a handler. The list is a
template argument pack, so the table's \emph{shape} is fixed when the
program is compiled. The \emph{decision} of which clause fires, however, is
made at run time by comparing the incoming key against the clause keys.
This distinction is the single most important thing to understand about the
facility, and it is easy to get wrong, so it is worth stating plainly
before going any further.

\begin{property}[Compile-time shape, run-time decision]
The set of clauses, the type of the key, the type of the result and the
types of the handlers are all determined at compile time. Which clause
handles a given key is determined at run time, by comparing the key value
against the compile-time key of each clause in turn. A table is therefore
\emph{not} a compile-time dispatch: adding a runtime value to the key does
not make the table dynamic, and removing a clause from the table does not
make the dispatch cheaper.
\end{property}

\section{The clause types}

The facility occupies lines 6293--6483 of \texttt{DSLtk.hpp} and consists
of four pieces: a clause template for keyed dispatch, a clause template for
the fallback, the table itself, and the free functions that build them.

A keyed clause is \api{dsl::WhenClause<Key, F>}, declared at line 6305:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp]
template <auto Key, typename F> struct WhenClause
{
  F handler;
  using handler_type_or_void = F;
  explicit constexpr WhenClause (F f) : handler (std::move (f)) {}
  //  ... try_invoke ...
};
\end{lstlisting}

The key is a \emph{non-type template parameter}. That is the source of both
the facility's power and its restrictions. Because the key is part of the
type, it must be a constant expression, and its type must be structural. In
practice this means the key is one of:

\begin{itemize}
\item an integral constant, such as \code{dsl::when<1>};
\item an enumeration constant, such as \code{dsl::when<State::Idle>};
\item an object of a structural class type, such as
\code{dsl::pattern<"[0-9]+">}, which has no non-static data members and
is therefore usable as a constant.
\end{itemize}

The fallback clause is \api{dsl::OtherwiseClause<F>}, declared at line
6366. It has the same shape, minus the key, and its \code{try_invoke}
always returns \code{false}. The fallback is therefore never found by the
ordinary scan; it is located by a second pass that looks for the
\code{OtherwiseClause} specialisation by type.

The table is \api{dsl::MatchTable<Clauses...>}, declared at line 6409. It
holds a \code{std::tuple} of the clauses and provides the call operator
that performs the dispatch.

\section{The factories}

Three free functions build the pieces. \code{dsl::when<Key>(f)} at line
6349 produces a \code{WhenClause}; \code{dsl::otherwise(f)} at line 6384
produces an \code{OtherwiseClause}; and \code{dsl::match(clauses...)} at
line 6478 produces the table.

\code{dsl::match} is a variadic template that decays its arguments and
constructs a \code{MatchTable} directly:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp]
template <typename... Clauses>
constexpr auto
match (Clauses &&...cs)
{
  return MatchTable<std::decay_t<Clauses>...>{ std::forward<Clauses> (cs)... };
}
\end{lstlisting}

A deduction guide is also provided at line 6465,

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp]
template <typename... Cs> MatchTable (Cs...) -> MatchTable<Cs...>;
\end{lstlisting}

so that a user who prefers to name the table type may write
\code{dsl::MatchTable table { clause1, clause2 };} and have the clause
types deduced. The guide is redundant with the factory for most uses, but
it makes \code{MatchTable} usable in a deduction context.

\section{How a key is recognised}

The heart of the facility is \code{WhenClause::try_invoke}, at lines
6311--6337. Its logic is a two-way choice made at compile time, on the
\emph{type} of the clause's key, and executed at run time on the
\emph{value} of the incoming key.

The first branch is taken when the key's type has a static member
\code{matches} taking a \code{std::string_view}. The detection is written
as a requirement:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp]
using KeyT = std::remove_cvref_t<decltype (Key)>;
if constexpr (requires { KeyT::matches (std::string_view{}); })
  {
    if constexpr (std::convertible_to<K, std::string_view>)
      {
        if (KeyT::matches (std::string_view (key)))
          {
            out = handler (key, std::forward<Args> (args)...);
            return true;
          }
      }
  }
\end{lstlisting}

The requirement is satisfied by \api{dsl::pattern<S>}, whose
\code{matches} member is the compile-time regex engine of
\chapref{ch:pattern-regex}. When it is satisfied, the incoming key must
also be convertible to a \code{std::string_view}; if it is not, the clause
cannot match and is skipped.

The second branch is taken otherwise, and requires that the incoming key
type and the clause's key type be equality comparable:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp]
else if constexpr (std::equality_comparable_with<K, decltype (Key)>)
  {
    if (key == Key)
      {
        out = handler (key, std::forward<Args> (args)...);
        return true;
      }
  }
\end{lstlisting}

If neither branch applies --- because the key type has no \code{matches}
member and is not comparable with the incoming type --- the clause simply
never matches, and the table behaves as though the clause were absent.

\begin{notebox}[A note on the \code{when<pattern<...>>} spelling]
The header's own documentation writes the pattern form as
\code{dsl::when<dsl::pattern<"[0-9]+">>(handler)}. That spelling does not
compile: \code{Key} is a non-type template parameter, so the argument must
be a \emph{value}, and a bare type name is not a value. Two spellings do
work. The first is to name the pattern in a constant:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp]
constexpr auto number_key = dsl::pattern<"[0-9]+">{};
auto table = dsl::match (dsl::when<number_key> (handler), ...);
\end{lstlisting}

The second is to parenthesise the value, which also disambiguates the
closing angle brackets:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp]
auto table = dsl::match (dsl::when<(dsl::pattern<"[0-9]+">{})> (handler), ...);
\end{lstlisting}
\end{notebox}

\section{The handler's arguments}

A handler is always called as \code{handler(key, extra...)}. The key
comes first, followed by whatever additional arguments the table was
called with. This is a small but consequential convention: it means a
handler always has access to the value that selected it, which is what
allows a single handler to be written for several keys.

It also means that the header's own ``Example B'' comment, at line 290 of
\texttt{DSLtk.hpp}, does not compile as written. That example writes
handlers of one argument, \code{[](auto e) { ... }}, and then calls the
table with a key and an event, \code{transitions(state, e)}. The handler
must accept both arguments:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp]
dsl::when<State::Idle> (
    [] (State, std::string_view e)
    { return e == "start" ? State::Running : State::Idle; })
\end{lstlisting}

The \code{otherwise} handler must accept the same number of arguments as
the keyed handlers, for the same reason: the table's return type is
computed from \code{std::invoke_result_t} over \emph{all} clauses, and
the fallback is included in that computation.

\section{The result type}

The return type of a table is computed at the point where the table's call
operator is instantiated. The relevant line is 6420:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp]
using RetT = std::common_type_t<std::invoke_result_t<
    typename Clauses::handler_type_or_void, Key, Args...>...>;
\end{lstlisting}

The type of every clause's handler is instantiated with the key type and
the extra argument types, and the common type of all those results becomes
the table's result type. A \code{std::optional<RetT>} holds the result
while the scan is in progress, and the value is unwrapped and returned at
the end.

This has two consequences that are easy to trip over. The first is that
all the handlers must be invocable with the same argument list. A handler
that takes the key but not the extra arguments produces a
\code{std::invoke_result} with no \code{type} member, and the error is
reported deep inside the call operator's instantiation.

The second is stricter than the common-type alias suggests. Each clause's
\code{try_invoke} takes an optional reference specialized on that handler's
concrete result type. It cannot bind an \code{optional<RetT>&} when the
specializations differ. For example, the common type of \code{std::string}
and \code{const char *} is \code{std::string}, but the pointer-returning
clause still fails this reference requirement. Return the same concrete type
from every handler, including the fallback.

\begin{notebox}[Uniform return types]
The rule of thumb is simple: give every handler in a table the same return
type, and make that type the same for the fallback as for the keyed
clauses. A table that returns \code{std::string} everywhere is the easiest
to work with, because the common type of a set of \code{std::string}
results is \code{std::string}.
\end{notebox}

\section{Dispatch}

The pseudocode block that follows states the algorithm.

\begin{pseudocode}[Dispatch on a key]
\textbf{function} \code{dispatch}(table, key, args)\\
\quad \textbf{for} each clause c of table, in declaration order \textbf{do}\\
\qquad \textbf{if} c is a \code{when} clause \textbf{then}\\
\qquad\qquad \textbf{if} the type of c.key has a static
\code{matches(string_view)} \textbf{then}\\
\qquad\qquad\qquad \textbf{if} \code{c.key.matches(key)} \textbf{then}\\
\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad \textbf{return} \code{c.handler(key, args)}\\
\qquad \textbf{else} \textbf{if} c.key is comparable with key \textbf{then}\\
\qquad\qquad \textbf{if} \code{key == c.key} \textbf{then}\\
\qquad\qquad\qquad \textbf{return} \code{c.handler(key, args)}\\
\quad \textbf{if} table contains an \code{otherwise} clause o \textbf{then}\\
\qquad \textbf{return} \code{o.handler(key, args)}\\
\quad \textbf{raise} \code{std::runtime_error}\\
\qquad \code{"dsl::match: no clause matched and no otherwise"}
\end{pseudocode}

Three properties of this algorithm are worth naming.

The \emph{first matching clause wins}. The implementation folds over the
clauses with a short-circuiting logical-or, so the scan stops at the first
clause whose \code{try_invoke} returns \code{true}. The order of the
clauses is therefore significant, and it is the order in which they were
written.

The \emph{fallback is not part of the scan}. \code{OtherwiseClause}'s
\code{try_invoke} unconditionally returns \code{false}, so the fallback
never terminates the scan. A second pass over the clause list finds it by
type. As a consequence the fallback clause may appear \emph{anywhere} in
the list, not only at the end, and if two fallback clauses are present the
first one in the list is the one that is used.

A table with \emph{no fallback and no match} throws. The exception is a
\code{std::runtime_error} whose message is exactly
\code{"dsl::match: no clause matched and no otherwise"}. The throw happens
after the scan and after the fallback pass, and only when no clause
produced a result and no fallback exists.

