\section{What the facility is for}

It is worth being explicit about when a match table is the right tool,
because it is not always the right tool.

Against a \code{switch}, the table wins in two ways. The first is
completeness: a \code{switch} over an enumeration that the compiler does
not know is exhaustive produces a warning, not an error, and a \code{switch}
over an integer has no notion of exhaustiveness at all; a table's clause
list is a single place where the set of handled values is written down.
The second is composability: because a clause is a self-contained object
with a key and a handler, clauses can be built by different functions and
combined, and a table can be assembled from them at run time.

Against a hash map from keys to handlers, the table wins when the number
of keys is small and known, and when the keys are of a type that can be
compared with \code{operator==} or tested with a \code{matches} member. It
loses when the key set is large, when the keys are strings that must be
hashed, or when the key set is not known until run time --- because a
clause's key is a template argument and therefore a constant. For a
dispatch over a hundred string commands, a \code{std::unordered_map} is
the right structure and a match table is not.

Against a chain of \code{if}-statements, the table wins in readability
and in the single point of change: adding a case means adding a clause,
and removing a case means removing a clause, with no risk of leaving a
stale \code{break} behind.

\section{Tables in constant expressions}

Because the whole dispatch machinery is usable in a constant expression, a
table can be a \code{static constexpr} member of a DSL class and can be
consulted during compilation. The worked example below does exactly that:
\code{StateMachine::transitions} and \code{LexerDSL::classify} are
\code{static constexpr} members, and the compiler evaluates them when it
instantiates the class.

The property that makes this work is that the pieces the call operator
uses are all constant-expression friendly in \cpp{}20: \code{std::optional}
and \code{std::apply} are \code{constexpr}, the clause tuple is a
\code{constexpr}-constructible \code{std::tuple}, and the
\code{std::string_view} comparisons the equality branch performs are
constant expressions. The one statement in the call operator that is not
usable in a constant expression is the \code{throw}, and a throw that is
not reached does no harm: the branch is simply never taken.

The practical benefit is that a table built at compile time costs nothing
at run time. There is no initialisation, no allocation and no indirection:
the table is a \code{std::tuple} of closures that the compiler has already
laid out, and calling it is a sequence of inline comparisons.

