\section{Interaction with the pattern facility}

A \code{dsl::pattern<...>} key turns the table into a classifier. Because
the pattern's \code{matches} member is a \code{constexpr} function, the
whole dispatch can be evaluated during constant evaluation when the key is
constant.

There is a subtlety in how pattern keys interact with the equality
branch. The detection in \code{try_invoke} is a requirement on the
\emph{type} of the key: \code{requires { KeyT::matches(std::string_view{});
}}. A \code{dsl::pattern} has such a member, so the pattern branch is
taken. But the branch additionally requires the incoming key type to be
convertible to a \code{std::string_view}. If the incoming key is an
\code{int}, the pattern branch is taken and then immediately abandoned,
because the inner \code{if constexpr} is false; the \code{else if constexpr}
branch is \emph{not} reached, since the outer \code{if constexpr} has
already committed to the pattern branch. The consequence is that a table
with a pattern key and an integer key cannot dispatch on the integer, and
mixing the two kinds of key in one table is a mistake.

Enumeration keys use the equality branch and are entirely straightforward.
The clause key \code{State::Idle} is an enumeration constant, and the
comparison \code{key == Key} is an ordinary equality comparison between two
values of the same enumeration type.

\section{A worked example}

Listing~\ref{lst:match-complete} contains two tables. The first is a state
machine whose key is an enumeration and whose extra argument is an event
name; it is the corrected form of the header's ``Example B''. The second
classifies a string against two pattern keys.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp, caption={A complete program: an
enumeration-keyed state machine and a pattern-keyed classifier, together
with the error raised by a table that has no fallback.},
label={lst:match-complete}]
#include "DSLtk.hpp"

#include <iostream>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <string>
#include <string_view>

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  A state machine, dispatched on an enumeration key
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
struct StateMachine : dsl::DSL<StateMachine, dsl::Pipeline, dsl::PatternMatch>
{
  enum class State
  {
    Idle,
    Running,
    Done
  };

  //  The handler receives the key first, then the extra arguments.
  static constexpr auto transitions = dsl::match (
      dsl::when<State::Idle> (
          [] (State, std::string_view e)
          { return e == "start" ? State::Running : State::Idle; }),
      dsl::when<State::Running> (
          [] (State, std::string_view e)
          { return e == "stop" ? State::Done : State::Running; }),
      dsl::otherwise (
          [] (State, std::string_view) { return State::Done; }));

  State state = State::Idle;

  void
  send (std::string_view e)
  {
    state = transitions (state, e);
  }

  //  The pipeline feature is orthogonal; it is used here only to format
  //  the state for display.
  std::string
  describe () const
  {
    return state
           | dsl::pipe ([] (State s)
                        { return "state "
                                 + std::to_string (static_cast<int> (s)); });
  }
};

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  A lexer, dispatched on a string key
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
constexpr auto number_key = dsl::pattern<"[0-9]+">{};
constexpr auto word_key = dsl::pattern<"[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z_0-9]*">{};

struct LexerDSL : dsl::DSL<LexerDSL, dsl::PatternMatch>
{
  static constexpr auto classify = dsl::match (
      dsl::when<number_key> (
          [] (std::string_view k) { return std::string{ "Number(" } + std::string{ k } + ")"; }),
      dsl::when<word_key> (
          [] (std::string_view k) { return std::string{ "Ident(" } + std::string{ k } + ")"; }),
      dsl::otherwise (
          [] (std::string_view k) { return std::string{ "Other(" } + std::string{ k } + ")"; }));
};

int
main ()
{
  StateMachine sm;
  sm.send ("start");
  std::cout << "after start: " << sm.describe () << '\n';
  sm.send ("stop");
  std::cout << "after stop:  " << sm.describe () << '\n';

  for (auto const *text : { "123", "alpha_7", "@" })
    std::cout << LexerDSL::classify (text) << '\n';

  auto strict = dsl::match (dsl::when<1> ([] (int k) { return k * 10; }));
  try
    {
      strict (2);
    }
  catch (const std::runtime_error &e)
    {
      std::cout << "caught: " << e.what () << '\n';
    }
}
\end{lstlisting}

The program prints

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp]
after start: state 1
after stop:  state 2
Number(123)
Ident(alpha_7)
Other(@)
caught: dsl::match: no clause matched and no otherwise
\end{lstlisting}

Note that the two tables in the listing are \code{static constexpr} members
of their DSL classes. This works because the whole dispatch machinery is
usable in a constant expression: the \code{std::optional} that holds the
result, the \code{std::apply} that walks the clause tuple, and the
\code{throw} that is the last resort are all constant-expression friendly
in \cpp{}20. A table built at compile time and stored as a member costs
nothing at run time.

\section{The feature tag}

The tag \api{dsl::PatternMatch} is declared at line 6503, and its mixin is
empty:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp]
struct PatternMatch
{
  template <typename Derived> struct Mixin
  {
    // Feature presence marker; no additional methods needed since
    // dsl::match/when/otherwise are free functions in the dsl:: namespace.
  };
};
\end{lstlisting}

The reason is the same as for \code{dsl::EnumReflection}: the API is a set
of free functions in namespace \code{dsl}, and there is nothing for a mixin
to contribute. The tag exists so that a DSL class can list pattern
matching among its features, which is documentation for the reader and a
place for a future extension to hook into.

\section{Edge cases and limits}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{All handlers must share a common return type.} The table's
result type is \code{std::common_type_t} over the
\code{std::invoke_result_t} of every clause, including the fallback. A
table whose keyed handlers return \code{std::string} and whose fallback
returns \code{const char *} does not compile because its clause expects a
different optional-reference specialization, despite a valid common type. The
error is reported deep inside the table's call operator, which makes it
easy to misdiagnose. Make the return types uniform.

\item \textbf{Every handler must accept the key plus every extra
argument.} A handler of the wrong arity is a hard error at the point where
the table is instantiated, and the diagnostic names the clause rather than
the user's lambda.

\item \textbf{The key must be a compile-time constant.} A runtime value
cannot be a clause key. A table that must dispatch on runtime data needs a
different structure, such as a \code{std::unordered_map} of handlers.

\item \textbf{Pattern keys and non-string keys cannot be mixed in one
table.} As explained above, the pattern branch of \code{try_invoke}
commits at compile time and is not revisited.

\item \textbf{A predicate cannot be a clause key.} The key is compared by
equality or by a static \code{matches} member, and a predicate has
neither; such a clause compiles but never matches. See
\chapref{ch:operators}.

\item \textbf{The dispatch is linear.} The clauses are examined in order,
so a table with $n$ clauses may perform $n$ comparisons. For a large table
of string keys this is a linear scan rather than a hash lookup, and the
facility offers no way to build a hashed table from the same clauses.

\item \textbf{There is no fall-through and no \code{break}.} A handler
returns a value; it cannot ask for the scan to continue. A table with
several clauses that may all apply must be expressed as a single handler
that decides internally.

\item \textbf{The exception is a \code{std::runtime_error}, not a
\code{dsl::Result}.} A table that may fail should model the failure in its
return type instead, and use a fallback clause to produce the failure
value. The throwing behaviour is the last resort, and it exists so that a
table without a fallback is not silently wrong.

\item \textbf{No support for capturing state in the table.} The clauses
are stored by value in a tuple, and the table is copyable, but there is no
mechanism for a table to be parameterised at run time beyond the
arguments passed to the call. A rule set that changes between runs must be
rebuilt.

\item \textbf{The return type of a table with no clauses is
\code{void}.} The common type of an empty pack of invoke results is
\code{void}, and the table's call operator would then attempt to
dereference an empty optional. A table with no clauses is not useful.
\end{itemize}

\section{Closing}

The pattern-match facility gives \DSLtk{} a small, typed dispatch table
whose shape is checked by the compiler and whose decisions are made at run
time. Its most valuable property is that the key and the handler are
bound together in a single compile-time unit, so a table cannot fall out of
step with the set of values it is meant to handle.

The next chapter looks at the facility that makes one kind of key
especially useful: \api{dsl::pattern}, the compile-time regular expression
engine that lets a clause key describe a \emph{set} of strings rather than
a single one.
